# GitHub Pages site

The repository contains a static Jekyll documentation site under `docs/` and a
workflow (`.github/workflows/pages.yml`) that publishes it to GitHub Pages.

## How it works

- Jekyll builds from `./docs` (source) to `./_site` (destination) using
  `actions/jekyll-build-pages`, then deploys via `actions/deploy-pages`.
- The workflow triggers **only on pushes to `develop`** (the integration branch
  and source of truth for `docs/`). `main` historically carried a stale
  `docs/_config.yml` (baseurl `/despacho-udea`, old branding) that published a
  broken site — do not re-add `main` to the trigger without syncing `main/docs`
  first (see the promote-develop-to-main chore).
- `baseurl` is `/gridforge`; the site lives at
  https://jccamargo94.github.io/gridforge/.
- The site uses the GridForge brand: `docs/assets/logo.svg` + `assets/main.css`,
  `_layouts/default.html` and `_includes/{header,footer}.html`. Keep this
  branding on any page you add or edit.

## Expectations

- Keep `docs/index.md` as the landing page with `layout: default` front matter,
  and ensure it links to the main docs pages.
- Every page under `docs/` needs front matter `title` + `layout: default`
  (Jekyll renders pages without a layout as bare markdown, missing the brand
  header/footer).
- Add or update a page under `docs/` whenever a user-facing capability, workflow,
  or mathematical formulation changes.
- Keep the site self-contained and avoid referencing unpublished features as if
  they were already implemented.
- Validate the build locally before pushing:
  `docker run --rm -v "$PWD/docs:/site" -w /site ruby:3.3 bash -c "gem install jekyll --no-document >/dev/null && jekyll build --baseurl /gridforge"`.
  `docs/_site/` is git-ignored.
- When the deployment workflow changes, verify that `docs/` is still the publish
  path and the baseurl/`relative_url` links still resolve.
